From: DroidDeck-Mali
Date: 2026-10-14
Subject: panvk: advertise VK_EXT_physical_device_drm when the node's numbers are known

0005 fills drm.render_rdev/drm.primary_rdev from the kbase node's st_rdev, and
the DRM properties are computed from those values - but the extension itself is
a hard-coded false in this fork's physical-device extension table, so the
properties are never readable and every rdev matcher still sees nothing.
Measured on the X6739 (MT6893, Mali-G77, arch 9, JM) with the driver build that
carries 0005: the session log still says

  [gamescope] [Warn] vulkan: physical device doesn't support VK_EXT_physical_device_drm
  PANVKDBG WSI: uses_kbase=1 termux_raw=0 raw_dri3=0 kbase_dmabuf=0 sw_device=1 ...

which is exactly the state 0005 was meant to change, and it cannot: with the
extension unadvertised, VkPhysicalDeviceProperties2 never reaches
panvk_get_physical_device_properties and the numbers stay invisible to the WSI.

Derive the flag from the same values the properties are. Reporting a device and
offering the extension that carries its device numbers are the same statement;
leaving them disagreed is what made 0005 a no-op. The bionic half still sees
both rdevs at zero (its DRM numbers come from the platform, not this path), so
the flag stays off there without needing the __ANDROID__ guard that 0005 uses.

The extension table is built after the kbase initializer runs - the rdev
assignment is at panvk_physical_device.c:1300, get_physical_device_extensions is
dispatched later in the same function - so the values are already there when the
table is filled.

--- a/src/panfrost/vulkan/panvk_vX_physical_device.c
+++ b/src/panfrost/vulkan/panvk_vX_physical_device.c
@@ -195,7 +195,13 @@ panvk_per_arch(get_physical_device_extensions)(
       .EXT_non_seamless_cube_map = true,
       .EXT_mutable_descriptor_type = PAN_ARCH >= 9,
       .EXT_multisampled_render_to_single_sampled = true,
-      .EXT_physical_device_drm = false,
+      /* Reporting the node's device numbers is only useful if the extension is
+       * actually offered, and the two properties are read only through it. The
+       * kbase initializer is what fills them in, and only on glibc: the bionic
+       * half puts frames on a real Android surface and leaves them zero, so this
+       * stays off there without needing a build-time guard of its own. */
+      .EXT_physical_device_drm =
+         device->drm.primary_rdev != 0 || device->drm.render_rdev != 0,
       .EXT_pipeline_creation_cache_control = true,
       .EXT_pipeline_creation_feedback = true,
       .EXT_pipeline_robustness = true,
